      "writtenOn": "2026-07-29T08:12:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is `pool_cache_is_free_index()` required by a concrete production consumer? It appears to introduce much of the current complexity: because entries held in a thread-local cache remain allocated from the backing pool, supporting logical \"free\" queries requires separate per-slot state and atomic transitions.\n\nWithout that API, the implementation could rely much more directly on fixed-size regular pools:\n\n- regular pools provide the global freelists, free bitmaps, allocation, and ASan behavior under the spinlock;\n- refill transfers indices from `pool_get()` into a bounded per-thread stack;\n- flush returns excess indices with `pool_put_index()`;\n- local put/get only poison and unpoison the payload;\n- another fixed-size backing pool is created under the lock when needed.\n\nThis removes both `next_free[]` and production `slot_state[]`, along with the production CAS. Double-free checking could remain debug-only if desired. Could we first confirm whether `pool_cache_is_free_index()` is actually needed before carrying its cost and semantics into the primitive?",
      "parentUuid": "6e32784e_4de4d526",
      "revId": "6bcff345b8d8665b171d2b53e26bf9017f359475",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c7e8becc_41e6f452",
        "filename": "src/vlib/pool_cache.h",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 217,
      "author": {
        "id": 361
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-29T08:12:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could this API use the same vocabulary as regular pools? In particular, `pool_cache_alloc()`/`pool_cache_free()` currently introduce a second naming convention for pool operations. Something like:\n\n```c\nu32 index \u003d pool_cache_get_index (\u0026cache);\npool_cache_put_index (\u0026cache, index);\npool_cache_free (\u0026cache); /* release resources */\n```\n\nwould align with `pool_get`, `pool_put_index`, `pool_free`, and `pool_elt_at_index`. If the shorter `pool_cache_get()` is preferred, its index-returning semantics should be explicit because regular `pool_get()` yields an element pointer through an output argument.",
      "revId": "6bcff345b8d8665b171d2b53e26bf9017f359475",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
